-- title:
--   Toric and finite p-torsion: product of orders equals #J[p]
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a positive integer $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit whose image under reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$, with $M/p$ nonzero, and a set $S$ of natural numbers. Assume the Hecke–diamond input predicate `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`, namely that `HeckeInputsHAlong (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) M H ℓ` holds for every prime $\ell$ and that every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ is realised by an automorphism $\sigma$ of the base-changed function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `IsDiamondAutHBar M H d σ`. Let $Pl$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $Pl$ and with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, and assume the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`. Let $\mathfrak{X}$ be integral model data `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` for the two-chart models of levels $\Gamma_H(M)$ and $\Gamma_{H}(M)\cap\Gamma_0$-type level `ΓN p M H hpM` over $\mathbb{Z}$ localised at $p$, let $\Lambda$ be level data (a group-law scheme over `base p` with bijections of its generic and special sections onto $J_H(M/p)$-type points and onto $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the residual function field), and let $O$ be a Néron object `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`: a smooth, separated, surjective, quasi-compact scheme $G \to$ `base p` with preconnected fibres carrying a commutative relative group law, a Galois- and Hecke-compatible bijection of its generic sections with $J = J_H(M)$, flat surjective multiplication by $n$, and toric data. Assume that $O.G$ with the unit section of its group law represents the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles on the level-$\Gamma_H(M)$ model that are fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero, and likewise that $\Lambda.X$ represents that subfunctor for the level-`ΓN` model, rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$. Then the toric subgroup $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,p$ (generated by the toric points at level $p$) and the finite subgroup $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$ (generated by the $p$-torsion classes whose associated generic section extends over $Pl$ along $\Lambda.\sigma_A$) are both contained in the $p$-torsion subgroup of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \mathrm{xHFunctionFieldBar}\,M\,H)$, and the product of their cardinalities equals the cardinality of that $p$-torsion subgroup.
--
--   This is Grothendieck's orthogonality for the toric and finite parts of the $p$-torsion of the Jacobian at a place of multiplicative reduction, recorded here in counted form: $\#J[p]^{\mathrm{tor}} \cdot \#J[p]^{\mathrm{fin}} = \#J[p]$, with both parts shown to be $p$-torsion. It feeds the later computation of the orthogonality of these two subgroups under the Weil pairing and the counting of the corresponding pieces of the $p$-torsion used in the level-lowering argument at the prime $p$ exactly dividing the level.
